WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:04.970
Bob Barker was 99 years old when he passed
away in 2023.

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:09.809
He spent more than half of those years hosting
TV game shows like "The Price is Right."

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:14.990
But his off-screen life had twists and turns
you'd never guess – like an unexpected bond

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:16.960
with Chuck Norris.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.660
Bob Barker's mother, Matilda Tarleton Barker,
was a school teacher on the Rosebud Native

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:26.779
American reservation in Mission, South Dakota,
and her husband was one-quarter Sioux.

00:00:26.779 --> 00:00:31.840
That meant Barker himself was one-eighth Sioux,
and was an enrolled member of the tribe, spending

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.590
many of his formative years on the reservation.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:39.430
When he was just six years old, his father,
an electrical power foreman, died in an accident

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:40.480
on the job.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:45.960
In the late 1930s, Barker and his mother relocated
to Springfield, Missouri, but he never forgot

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.360
either his Native American or his South Dakotan
roots.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.430
He told the Argus Leader in 1962,

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:56.260
"I've always bragged about being part Indian
because they are a people to be proud of.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:58.980
And the Sioux were the greatest warriors of
them all."

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:03.140
Barker loved talking about his heritage and
enjoyed telling stories about the Sioux's

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:08.430
legendary prowess in battle and their place
in history, with his own singular wit.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:09.430
He joked,

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:13.860
"They've been called the greatest light cavalry
in the history of man, and I have never been

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.000
on a horse without falling off."

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.180
As for South Dakota, they're just as proud
of him as he was of them.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:24.939
In 1980, Barker was inducted into the state's
Hall of Fame in the Arts and Entertainment

00:01:24.939 --> 00:01:26.369
category.

00:01:26.369 --> 00:01:29.690
Growing up in Missouri, Bob Barker always
wanted to be an athlete.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:34.680
He had a particular obsession with one day
playing for his hometown's major league baseball

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:35.680
team.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:39.000
"I wanted to pitch for the St. Louis Cardinals.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.409
I was held back by a total lack of talent."

00:01:41.409 --> 00:01:47.590
Eventually, he went to college on a basketball
scholarship, but gave up on his hopes of ever

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:48.969
being a pro athlete.

00:01:48.969 --> 00:01:52.719
Over the years, though, Barker often talked
about his childhood dreams of playing for

00:01:52.719 --> 00:01:56.360
the Cardinals, and once he was famous, the
club took notice.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:01.680
In a 2000 interview, Barker said that then-Cardinals
manager Whitey Herzog invited the host to

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.939
come visit the team when he was in St. Louis
for a show.

00:02:04.939 --> 00:02:09.790
Herzog gave Barker his very own Cardinals
team jacket, and team management also made

00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:11.190
his dream come true.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:12.190
Barker said,

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:13.890
"The Cardinals signed me to a contract.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:19.489
I get a dollar a year from the St. Louis Cardinals
so long as I do not pitch."

00:02:19.489 --> 00:02:23.360
Many viewers became familiar with Bob Barker
as the host of "The Price is Right" in the

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:25.470
'80s, '90s, and beyond.

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:32.379
Indeed, between 1972 and 2007, there were
well over 6,700 episodes of the famous game

00:02:32.379 --> 00:02:36.010
show, and with just four exceptions, Barker
hosted them all.

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:40.379
But the truth is, Barker was already a household
name when he offered up his first Showcase

00:02:40.379 --> 00:02:42.310
in 1972.

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:46.660
Long before he was telling contestants they'd
gone over with their bids, Barker was the

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:51.580
host of another wildly successful game show,
"Truth or Consequences," where he'd been brought

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:55.650
in to replace outgoing host Jack Bailey in
1956.

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:59.879
He continued hosting that show for 18 years,
during which time he became one of the most

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:01.510
famous game show hosts on television.

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:03.239
"It was nice to have you join us there at
home for 'Truth or Consequences.'

00:03:03.239 --> 00:03:06.239
Make it a habit, won't you?"

00:03:06.239 --> 00:03:10.750
Even before "Truth or Consequences," Barker
had already made a name for himself elsewhere.

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:15.099
Prior to his game show days, he appeared on
the radio, hosting "The Bob Barker Show" from

00:03:15.099 --> 00:03:17.280
a studio in Burbank, California.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.670
He also hosted what was then called an "audience
participation show."

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:25.140
The story goes that it was hearing "The Bob
Barker Show" on the radio that convinced TV

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:31.050
producer Ralph Edwards to select Barker to
be the next host of "Truth or Consequences."

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:36.260
In 1945, Bob Barker married his high school
sweetheart, Dorothy Jo Gideon.

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:41.049
Over the course of Baker's career, Gideon
served as a producer, writer, and assistant.

00:03:41.049 --> 00:03:45.040
According to the man himself, he owes everything
he's ever accomplished to her.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:46.910
He told the Television Academy Foundation,

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:50.299
"Without Dorothy Jo, you and I probably would
not be talking.

00:03:50.299 --> 00:03:53.799
[...] She gave me the confidence to even try
to do what I set out to do."

00:03:53.799 --> 00:03:58.820
Despite having no formal training, no experience,
and no background in entertainment, Gideon

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:01.799
believed Barker could make something of himself
in the industry.

00:04:01.799 --> 00:04:02.799
The host recalled,

00:04:02.799 --> 00:04:05.110
"Dorothy Jo said, 'You do this well enough.'

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:09.040
She said, 'You're going to be able to do this,'
and that gave me the confidence to try."

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:12.239
Gideon wasn't just Barker's emotional support,
though.

00:04:12.239 --> 00:04:16.209
After relocating to California, she left her
job as a school teacher and went to work with

00:04:16.209 --> 00:04:20.400
Barker full-time, writing commercial jingles
and much more.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:24.040
Barker explained that Gideon produced and
wrote for Barker's radio shows.

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:27.960
The couple remained together until Gideon's
death from cancer in 1981.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:33.630
"She was very bright and totally devoted."

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:38.750
Embarking on a career in radio thanks to the
support of his wife, Dorothy Jo Gideon, Bob

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:44.699
Barker got his first job out of college at
WWPG, a radio station in Palm Beach County,

00:04:44.699 --> 00:04:45.699
Florida.

00:04:45.699 --> 00:04:49.039
They'd moved there from Springfield, Missouri,
and he eventually found himself as an editor

00:04:49.039 --> 00:04:51.130
and staff announcer at the station.

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:55.040
But his favorite moments weren't the times
he was hosting shows on the air, but the holiday

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:57.850
season when he played a jolly old Saint Nick.

00:04:57.850 --> 00:04:59.940
Barker declared with absolute certainty,

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:03.070
"I was the greatest Santa Claus in the history
of radio.

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:06.850
[...] I would go over to this store and put
on my Santa Claus outfit and I lived it.

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:11.590
I loved it, I had more fun talking with those
kids, and their parents in some cases too,

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:13.280
and we got lots of laughs.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:16.100
We got mail from more adults than we did kids."

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:20.009
It was so much of a joy for Barker that he
said he would have made being Santa Claus

00:05:20.009 --> 00:05:23.820
his full-time job if Christmas was year round.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:27.590
When Bob Barker ended his run as the host
of "The Price is Right," it marked the end

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:31.789
of a 35-year era – a then-record for the
game show circuit.

00:05:31.789 --> 00:05:36.370
He wasn't the first host of the show, but
he was key to its success when it was relaunched

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:41.789
as "The New Price is Right" in 1972, though
Barker didn't know it at the time.

00:05:41.789 --> 00:05:45.670
Initially signed to what he felt was a very
generous contract, he learned later that the

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:47.820
show would not have happened without him.

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:49.970
Barker told the Television Academy Foundation,

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:55.199
"[Creator] Mark Goodson had talked with [producer]
Bud Grant, and Bud Grant had said 'Yes I'll

00:05:55.199 --> 00:05:58.949
buy that show for CBS if you get Bob Barker
to host it.'

00:05:58.949 --> 00:06:00.819
I didn't know that when we negotiated.

00:06:00.819 --> 00:06:04.069
If I'd known that, Mark could have been even
more generous than he was."

00:06:04.069 --> 00:06:05.970
But it all worked out for everyone.

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:10.170
"The Price is Right" went on to become one
of the most successful game shows of all time,

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:13.130
and Barker became a TV icon.

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:17.820
In the decades after he came to fame, Bob
Barker received numerous awards and honors,

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:23.630
including an incredible 19 Daytime Emmy Awards
and a Daytime Emmy Lifetime Achievement Award.

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:28.289
He also had a ship renamed in his honor, and
it serves a cause close to his heart.

00:06:28.289 --> 00:06:33.380
A whaling vessel was rechristened the Bob
Barker by Sea Shepherd Global and served as

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:36.280
an anti-whaling ship for 13 years.

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:39.979
There's also a street in his old hometown
of Springfield, Missouri, that boasts the

00:06:39.979 --> 00:06:41.790
name, Bob Barker Boulevard.

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:46.660
He received his star on the Hollywood Walk
of Fame all the way back in 1976.

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:52.440
But the honor that meant the most to him was
when CBS renamed Studio 33 "Bob Barker Studio"

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:54.620
in 1998.

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:59.009
Over 35 years of hosting "The Price is Right,"
there have been many unforgettable moments

00:06:59.009 --> 00:07:01.129
— for Barker, and for audiences.

00:07:01.129 --> 00:07:05.101
There was the time a contestant accidentally
saw the right price by mistake, or the time

00:07:05.101 --> 00:07:09.320
a game malfunctioned outright and gave the
contestant a win when it shouldn't have.

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:13.370
But for Bob, no moment can compare to the
time a woman got a little too excited when

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:17.660
her name was called, and her bouncing up and
down gave everybody a show after her tube

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:19.280
top couldn't hold her in.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:23.190
Barker has often talked about the incident
as one of his favorite moments as host of

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:27.970
"The Price is Right," including his appearance
on the "Rosie O'Donnell Show" in 1998.

00:07:27.970 --> 00:07:31.441
After showing a clip, he said that moment
was still the most talked-about in "Price

00:07:31.441 --> 00:07:35.120
is Right" history despite happening 20 years
earlier.

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:40.160
"She came on down and they came on out!"

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:44.729
Even before he was famous as the host of "Truth
or Consequences," Bob Barker had already been

00:07:44.729 --> 00:07:45.759
on TV.

00:07:45.759 --> 00:07:50.259
His first shot on the small screen was on
the game show "Your Big Moment," a local talent

00:07:50.259 --> 00:07:54.509
show that ran on an NBC affiliate in Los Angeles
for 13 episodes.

00:07:54.509 --> 00:07:57.789
It turns out, Barker was on TV before he even
owned one.

00:07:57.789 --> 00:08:02.389
The fact is, Barker broke into TV right when
it was just catching on as an entirely new

00:08:02.389 --> 00:08:05.290
medium that fascinated the public's imagination.

00:08:05.290 --> 00:08:07.150
He said to the Television Academy Foundation,

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:11.780
"I can remember driving around Hollywood here
and there were people gathered around in front

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:15.979
of the windows at the places where you could
buy television sets watching television ... And

00:08:15.979 --> 00:08:17.440
it just swept the country."

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:22.210
It was a radical new way of enjoying entertainment
at home, and it grew so fast that Barker knew

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:27.199
his career was going to be taking a very different
trajectory than he'd first imagined, saying,

00:08:27.199 --> 00:08:31.960
"When I first came out here [in 1950] I was
interested in a national radio show ... but

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:36.900
by '52, probably, I knew that if I were going
to work it was going to be on television on

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:39.209
a national scale."

00:08:39.209 --> 00:08:42.959
Considering that the image most people have
in their minds of Bob Barker is of a stiff-moving

00:08:42.959 --> 00:08:47.440
older man with white hair, it might raise
an eyebrow to find out that he was actually

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:49.260
a trained martial artist.

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:53.540
In fact, he trained under one of Hollywood's
most famous action heroes, none other than

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:55.560
Chuck Norris himself.

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:59.839
Even more unexpected is that Barker met Norris
years before the karate master embarked on

00:08:59.839 --> 00:09:02.140
his own Hollywood career.

00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:05.200
As it happens, Barker bumped into him when
he was on his earlier game show.

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:08.360
In an interview with the Television Academy
Foundation, he said,

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:11.649
"I had Chuck Norris as a guest on 'Truth or
Consequences.

00:09:11.649 --> 00:09:16.930
He did a karate demonstration, and I not only
had not done karate, I had never seen karate.

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:19.860
I was so impressed that I started taking lessons."

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:24.720
As Barker recounted, he and Chuck Norris formed
a years-long personal relationship that the

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:25.940
host looked back on fondly.

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:30.950
The arrangement also gave him an up-close
view at Norris' attempts to make it in show

00:09:30.950 --> 00:09:31.950
business.

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:33.590
Speaking about their time together, Barker
recalled,

00:09:33.590 --> 00:09:37.970
"He used to come over to my house and give
me a karate lesson, have a shower, and then

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:40.430
he'd go down the street to an acting class.

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:43.350
That was before he'd ever even made his first
movie, you know.

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:45.170
I worked with him for seven or eight years.

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:46.800
I had a great time with Chuck."

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:51.110
According to Barker, Norris didn't go easy
on him, revealing that in one session Norris

00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:54.860
kicked him, breaking two of the host's ribs.

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:59.630
Though Bob Barker is one of the most beloved
faces in TV history, he wasn't immune to the

00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:01.709
occasional controversy.

00:10:01.709 --> 00:10:05.720
During his many years on the show, numerous
allegations were made against Barker by women

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:08.010
accusing him of wrongful conduct.

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:13.529
In 1994, model Dian Parkinson brought a lawsuit
against Barker and "The Price is Right," claiming

00:10:13.529 --> 00:10:17.760
she had been coerced into having a sexual
relationship with the host in order to keep

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:18.760
her job.

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:23.209
While Barker initially denied their relationship,
he later acknowledged having an affair with

00:10:23.209 --> 00:10:24.209
Parkinson.

00:10:24.209 --> 00:10:28.220
According to Barker, the pair had engaged
in a consensual relationship while she was

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:32.180
working on the show, but insisted that there
was no merit to the charge that she'd been

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:35.130
pressured into it, calling the case "frivolous."

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:39.779
The suit, which Barker said only came about
after he had refused to pay for her silence,

00:10:39.779 --> 00:10:40.940
was eventually dropped.

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:43.750
"She's no fun at all, that Dian."

00:10:43.750 --> 00:10:47.940
Parkinson said she was out of money and was
suffering medical problems caused by the stress

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:49.149
of the case.

00:10:49.149 --> 00:10:52.860
Barker maintained his innocence and claimed
that if there were any chance she could win

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:55.680
in court, the case would never have been dismissed.

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:00.690
A year later, though, another suit was filed
against Barker by model Holly Hallstrom, alleging

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:04.399
that she had been fired from "The Price is
Right" for gaining weight.

00:11:04.399 --> 00:11:09.300
Barker filed a counter-claim of slander, but
the two sides reached a deal out of court.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:13.760
A well-known and staunch advocate for animal
rights, Bob Barker was involved with numerous

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:15.250
charities over the years.

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:18.209
He used his considerable platform to help
protect animals.

00:11:18.209 --> 00:11:23.180
"I was just born with a love of animals."

00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:27.750
In addition to urging viewers to have their
pets spayed and neutered, he refused to allow

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:30.630
fur of any kind as a prize on the show.

00:11:30.630 --> 00:11:35.750
Famously, Barker went vegetarian in the late
1970s, an era when that was decidedly not

00:11:35.750 --> 00:11:37.880
the norm for big celebrities.

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:43.370
Barker later spent $25 million to establish
the DJ&amp;T Foundation, which helps clinics spay

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:49.760
and neuter to deter pet euthanization, and
donated $5 million to the Sea Shepherd Conservation

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:50.760
Society.

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:54.910
Barker eventually joined PETA, but perhaps
his most impactful charitable endeavor came

00:11:54.910 --> 00:11:59.810
from something he didn't do when he refused
to continue hosting the Miss USA Pageant over

00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:01.680
an animal rights issue.

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:05.959
Barker claimed that the organization agreed
to discontinue the practice, but in 1988,

00:12:05.959 --> 00:12:10.550
he went to host and discovered that the plan
was for the contestants to walk out on stage

00:12:10.550 --> 00:12:13.370
in fur coats, and he decided to walk away.

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:17.570
He said leaving the pageant was the best thing
to ever happen for the anti-fur campaign.

00:12:17.570 --> 00:12:22.810
Overnight, news of his exit from the pageant
was all over television, and suddenly millions

00:12:22.810 --> 00:12:27.190
who had never known about the cause were becoming
aware of it for the first time.

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:31.740
The vision many have of Bob Barker today is
one of a stately older gentleman with pure

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:33.029
white hair.

00:12:33.029 --> 00:12:37.300
That hair was one of his most distinguishing
features for decades and set him apart from

00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:39.769
so many other game show presenters.

00:12:39.769 --> 00:12:44.720
But for decades before, Barker had sported
a suave, dark brown 'do, even well into his

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:45.720
60s.

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:49.150
That's because producers of "The Price is
Right" had Barker dyeing his hair, something

00:12:49.150 --> 00:12:53.649
which began to upset the host when he learned
that the product used was tested on animals.

00:12:53.649 --> 00:12:58.310
And it took a fight with folks behind the
scenes to let him sport his natural hair color.

00:12:58.310 --> 00:13:00.740
That day finally came in 1987.

00:13:00.740 --> 00:13:05.120
Arriving on the stage with a pure white head
of hair, the crowd roared with excitement.

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:07.070
He told talk show host Ross Shaffer,

00:13:07.070 --> 00:13:09.170
"The reaction has been very positive.

00:13:09.170 --> 00:13:10.649
The girls love this gray hair.

00:13:10.649 --> 00:13:14.040
Since my hair became gray there's just not
enough of me to go around."

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:18.630
Barker even claimed in an Los Angeles Times
interview in 1990 that ratings actually went

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:21.360
up after his hair went white.

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:25.380
During his time on "The Price is Right," Bob
Barker often talked about how much he loved

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:28.779
making the show saying that he'd host for
as long as it was fun.

00:13:28.779 --> 00:13:33.490
He continued hosting for nearly 20 more years
before he finally spun the showcase showdown

00:13:33.490 --> 00:13:36.870
wheel for the last time as the regular host
in 2007.

00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:40.779
Believe it or not, though, he almost said
goodbye seven years earlier.

00:13:40.779 --> 00:13:44.300
In an interview with the Television Academy
in 2000 he revealed,

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:48.290
"I thoroughly enjoy what I do, or I would
have retired long ago.

00:13:48.290 --> 00:13:49.680
But this is all I've ever done.

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:51.180
I was going to quit this year.

00:13:51.180 --> 00:13:54.980
The show was going beautifully, the ratings
are terrific, I'm doing as well as I've ever

00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:55.980
done, I think.

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:57.070
I should go out on top.

00:13:57.070 --> 00:13:58.610
I shouldn't stay too long.

00:13:58.610 --> 00:14:00.490
And so I was going to retire this year."

00:14:00.490 --> 00:14:04.220
Thankfully for his millions of fans, the network
wouldn't let him go.

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:08.850
They made him an offer he couldn't refuse,
and Barker re-signed for another year, ultimately

00:14:08.850 --> 00:14:12.060
adding another six seasons to his resume after
that.

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:16.750
They say records are made to be broken, and
TV records are no exception.

00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:21.290
For a long while it was Johnny Carson who
held the seemingly unbeatable record for continuous

00:14:21.290 --> 00:14:24.029
performances on the same network television
show.

00:14:24.029 --> 00:14:29.000
Then, in 2002, along came Bob Barker, who
one-upped the late-night legend by hosting

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:33.620
"The Price is Right" for 29 years, seven months,
and 22 days.

00:14:33.620 --> 00:14:38.230
Retiring officially in 2007, he'd continue
to hold the record for another seven years

00:14:38.230 --> 00:14:42.529
until longtime "Jeopardy" host Alex Trebek
took his crown in 2014.

00:14:42.529 --> 00:14:46.680
Trebek hosted his own show right up until
his death in 2020, with his final episodes

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:48.210
airing a year later.

00:14:48.210 --> 00:14:53.320
By 2023, the new record holder was Pat Sajak,
host of "Wheel of Fortune," who has hosted

00:14:53.320 --> 00:14:59.069
over an eye-popping 7,700 episodes across
four decades.

00:14:59.069 --> 00:15:04.220
Bob Barker was known for his amiable personality
and jovial wit, perhaps even as one of the

00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:05.839
happiest men in Hollywood.

00:15:05.839 --> 00:15:10.670
He was so hard to make angry that David Letterman
poked fun at his famously upbeat attitude

00:15:10.670 --> 00:15:13.779
with a "Top 10 List of Things That Make Bob
Barker Angry."

00:15:13.779 --> 00:15:18.050
"And the number one thing that makes Bob Barker
angry…"

00:15:18.050 --> 00:15:22.029
"Recent allegations that I'm fixed."

00:15:22.029 --> 00:15:27.529
But there was one person who really did draw
Barker's ire, and it's probably the last person

00:15:27.529 --> 00:15:30.920
you'd ever expect: "Golden Girls" star Betty
White.

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:35.860
The feud between the two celebs went down
back in 2009, two years after Barker had retired

00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:37.180
from "The Price is Right."

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:41.660
Now focused almost entirely on his animal
advocacy, Barker was involved in the very

00:15:41.660 --> 00:15:46.110
public fight to get an elephant in the L.A.
Zoo released into a sanctuary.

00:15:46.110 --> 00:15:50.420
But on the other side was White, a proponent
of the zoo who feared it would mean the end

00:15:50.420 --> 00:15:54.820
of the zoo industry, which itself helps raise
awareness of the plight of animals.

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:58.470
Eventually, the battle between the former
network stars got heated, with Barker rumored

00:15:58.470 --> 00:16:02.269
to have said he wouldn't attend the ceremony
for his own Lifetime Achievement Award at

00:16:02.269 --> 00:16:05.130
the Game Show Awards that year if White was
in attendance.

00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:09.300
Though neither ever commented publicly on
the spat, it's worth noting that White did

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:14.130
not attend in person, and Barker accepted
his award on stage.

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:19.110
Though Bob Barker did indeed retire from hosting
"The Price is Right" in 2007, it's not actually

00:16:19.110 --> 00:16:21.300
the final time he appeared on the show.

00:16:21.300 --> 00:16:23.839
In fact, it's not even the last time he hosted.

00:16:23.839 --> 00:16:28.240
In 2009, Barker popped up in a special guest
appearance, walking out during a showcase

00:16:28.240 --> 00:16:30.550
to promote his book, "Priceless Memories."

00:16:30.550 --> 00:16:35.079
Emerging to a thunderous applause, he shook
hands with his replacement Drew Carey, before

00:16:35.079 --> 00:16:39.620
gifting a copy of his book to every member
of the audience, and announcing that all profits

00:16:39.620 --> 00:16:43.959
from the sale of the book would go to his
non-profit DJ&amp;T Foundation.

00:16:43.959 --> 00:16:48.190
Six years later, as part of the show's annual
April Fool's tradition, "The Price is Right"

00:16:48.190 --> 00:16:52.060
introduced Drew Carey, only for Barker himself
to come out on stage.

00:16:52.060 --> 00:16:55.480
"I have never had a welcome like that!"

00:16:55.480 --> 00:16:59.700
Barker, at age 92, may have been a little
slower than fans were used to seeing him,

00:16:59.700 --> 00:17:04.630
but he was as sharp as ever in his final hosting
gig, handing the first bid and pricing game

00:17:04.630 --> 00:17:07.530
before handing the reins back over to Carey.

00:17:07.530 --> 00:17:12.130
"The Price is Right" won Bob Barker critical
acclaim and countless awards, and turned him

00:17:12.130 --> 00:17:13.780
into a cultural icon.

00:17:13.780 --> 00:17:19.170
In addition to his 19 Emmy wins, he was nominated
an additional 20 times during his career.

00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:24.820
But when perusing a list of all of his honors
there's one award that stands out: An MTV

00:17:24.820 --> 00:17:25.929
movie award.

00:17:25.929 --> 00:17:30.150
Not known for his on-screen acting, Barker
was a surprise choice for the award, which

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:34.780
was given for his bit role in the 1996 Adam
Sandler comedy "Happy Gilmore."

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:38.910
In the film, Sandler's Gilmore gets into a
fistfight with Barker, playing himself, at

00:17:38.910 --> 00:17:40.450
a celebrity golf tournament.

00:17:40.450 --> 00:17:44.470
It's one of the most memorable moments in
the film, and one that has Barker playing

00:17:44.470 --> 00:17:47.780
decidedly against type duking it out with
Sandler.

00:17:47.780 --> 00:17:50.940
The fight scene was also the origin of Sandler's
iconic line,

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:53.299
"The price is wrong, b—!"

00:17:53.299 --> 00:17:58.039
For this scene, he and Sandler took home the
MTV Movie Award for best fight.

00:17:58.039 --> 00:18:02.440
As the host of one of America's most popular
game shows for 35 years, you might think that

00:18:02.440 --> 00:18:06.340
Bob Barker loved being on the other end of
contestants playing the game, knowing the

00:18:06.340 --> 00:18:09.180
prizes and all the right answers before they
did.

00:18:09.180 --> 00:18:11.900
According to Barker though, that's not the
case at all.

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:14.820
In an interview with the Television Academy
Foundation, Barker said,

00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:16.650
"I don't know any of the prices.

00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:20.010
In fact, I don't even know what the prize
is going to be ... and in the games where

00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:23.690
the price is the all-important thing, I don't
know what the prices are.

00:18:23.690 --> 00:18:25.679
And I don't particularly want to."

00:18:25.679 --> 00:18:30.110
As Barker tells it, being in the dark allows
him to be a lot more interactive with his

00:18:30.110 --> 00:18:33.900
contestants, giving him the opportunity to
play along with them, have a lot more fun,

00:18:33.900 --> 00:18:35.490
and even tease them a bit.

00:18:35.490 --> 00:18:36.490
He said,

00:18:36.490 --> 00:18:39.559
"If I knew [a contestant] was going to lose,
that would be cruel in a way.

00:18:39.559 --> 00:18:42.740
But when I don't know I can play it to the
hilt."

00:18:42.740 --> 00:18:47.179
Retiring from "The Price is Right" in 2007,
and making his last hosting appearance in

00:18:47.179 --> 00:18:52.060
2015, Bob Barker spent the last years of his
life out of the public eye, popping up mostly

00:18:52.060 --> 00:18:54.929
to help promote important animal rights causes.

00:18:54.929 --> 00:18:59.110
Unfortunately, the last 20 or so years of
his life were marked with a number of health

00:18:59.110 --> 00:19:03.540
scares, beginning with frequent fights with
skin cancer, apparently caused by decades

00:19:03.540 --> 00:19:04.540
of tanning.

00:19:04.540 --> 00:19:08.419
The same year he returned to "The Price is
Right" as guest host, Barker also made the

00:19:08.419 --> 00:19:13.740
news for taking a fall and injuring his knee,
receiving a number of stitches and being hospitalized

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:14.910
as a result.

00:19:14.910 --> 00:19:18.929
Less than two years later, he was back in
the hospital again for another fall, and again

00:19:18.929 --> 00:19:20.400
a year after that.

00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:24.580
Though it wasn't always easy, Barker nevertheless
remained relatively active.

00:19:24.580 --> 00:19:29.500
He was often seen visiting his wife's grave
and was sometimes photographed by paparazzi

00:19:29.500 --> 00:19:33.750
while out on leisurely strolls near his Los
Angeles home.

00:19:33.750 --> 00:19:38.380
Even during his lifetime, there was never
any doubt that the first line in every obituary

00:19:38.380 --> 00:19:42.570
for Bob Barker would be about his role as
host of "The Price is Right."

00:19:42.570 --> 00:19:45.950
But that's not what Barker himself really
wanted to be remembered for.

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:51.300
In 2007, he spoke with interviewer Terry Morrow
of the Knoxville News Sentinel about his legacy,

00:19:51.300 --> 00:19:52.300
saying,

00:19:52.300 --> 00:19:56.549
"I think that it would be nice if people remembered
me as a guy who loved all living things and

00:19:56.549 --> 00:20:01.220
did as much as he could to make ours a better
world for animals — and also did quite a

00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:02.690
few television shows."

00:20:02.690 --> 00:20:06.260
Well, rest easy, Bob, because you just about
got your wish.

00:20:06.260 --> 00:20:10.630
While his role as host of "Price is Right"
did indeed top his memorials following his

00:20:10.630 --> 00:20:15.630
passing in August of 2023, it was his work
fighting for animal rights that was often

00:20:15.630 --> 00:20:17.700
mentioned right up there with it.

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:20.049
"Help control the pet population.

00:20:20.049 --> 00:20:22.870
Have your pets spayed or neutered."

